commit 0ec39fa3c03b5b87dab410dc3a2ed1b63ee84ec5
Author: Digital Trekkerr <support@digitaltrekkerr.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 17:18:36 2026 +0300

    Drop maxOutputWords, fix Gemini HTTP 400, restore built-in prompts, theme overlay
    
    Three user-visible fixes + one cleanup:
    
    • Drop maxOutputWords entirely
      - Removes the field from PromptTemplate and TranslationRequest, the
        max_tokens/maxOutputTokens lines from Gemini, OpenAI-compatible and
        OpenRouter providers, the editor UI, and the ARB keys.
      - The settings-level INPUT word limit (word_limitProvider) stays — that's
        the user-controlled cap on how much text is sent.
      - Providers no longer impose an output cap at all; the API uses its own
        default. gpt-5.x, OpenRouter free tier, and similar models that reject
        'max_tokens' now stop erroring.
      - Tests verify the absence of 'max_tokens' / 'maxOutputTokens' on the wire
        and that legacy JSON still loads (PromptTemplate.fromJson ignores the
        old 'maxOutputWords' key).
    
    • Fix Gemini HTTP 400 on non-thinking models
      - GeminiProvider previously sent 'thinkingConfig' to every Gemini model.
        Models outside the 2.5/3 family (e.g. Gemini 1.5, 2.0, Gemma) reject
        it with 400.
      - New _supportsThinkingConfig(model) helper matches '^gemini-(2\.5|3)'.
        thinkingConfig is only attached for those families. Restores the
        pre-V2 wire format that worked.
    
    • Fix template editor's 'Reset to Default'
      - The button used a hard-coded generic short prompt instead of the
        template's actual built-in prompt (e.g. the full Gemini Reconstruction
        formatting prompt was lost).
      - SettingsRepository now exposes builtInSystemPromptFor(id) and the
        built-in name list. The editor calls it for built-in templates; custom
        templates still reset to the local default.
      - 4 new tests cover the catalog and the unknown-id fallback.
    
    • Overlay theming follows app theme
      - The overlay is a separate Flutter engine with its own MaterialApp.
        Previously hardcoded ThemeData.dark() and 5 dark palette constants.
      - Now reads SharedPreferences 'theme_mode' (new public
        SettingsRepository.themeModeKey) and builds light/dark themes from the
        same #4F46E5 seed as the main app. The 5 palette constants became
        context-driven getters (Theme.of(context).colorScheme.{primary,
        surface, surfaceContainer, onSurface, onSurfaceVariant}).
      - Falls back to dark on absence or error to preserve historical look.
    
    Build: 0.1.5+2010 → 0.1.5+2011
    Tests: 289/289 passing across all packages (151 translation_core,
           52 translation_app, 52 file_handler, 23 markdown_renderer,
           11 history).

